course: biology
lesson: 176
title: The deep dark: anglerfish, giant squid, blobfish and whale falls
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Far below the twilight zone the sea is black, freezing cold and squeezed by huge pressure, and food is so rare that animals must catch anything that comes near.
explain: So the deep is full of clever tricks: an anglerfish dangles a glowing lure in front of its mouth like a fishing rod, and a gulper eel has a mouth so big it can swallow a meal almost its own size.
explain: When a whale dies and sinks, it is a feast that lasts for years: crabs, hagfish and sleeper sharks eat the meat, and special worms live only on the bones, like a whole town growing round a giant picnic.
explain: Most of the deep sea floor has never been seen by any person, which is why people say we have mapped the Moon better than the bottom of our own ocean.
fact: Deep-sea anglerfish have a glowing lure on their heads to attract prey, and in many kinds the light comes from glowing bacteria living inside the lure; many kinds are smaller than a football.
fact: Anglerfish and gulper eels can swallow prey as big as themselves or bigger, because their mouths open very wide and their stomachs stretch.
fact: The giant squid was filmed alive in its deep home for the first time in 2012, and its eyes are among the biggest of any animal, about as big as a football; like other squid it has eight arms and two longer tentacles.
fact: The vampire squid flips its webbed arms over its body like a cloak when threatened, and despite its name it eats drifting bits of dead stuff, not blood.
fact: Dumbo octopuses have fins that look like elephant ears, and they live deeper than any other known octopus, at nearly seven kilometres.
fact: Blobfish look like sad jelly blobs in photos because they are hurt when pulled up from the deep, but in their deep home they look like normal fish; a photo of a hurt blobfish once won a vote for the world's ugliest animal.
fact: When a whale dies and sinks to the deep sea floor, its body feeds animals for years, and bone-eating worms live only on whale bones.
fact: Hagfish make huge amounts of slime when attacked, enough to fill a bucket in seconds, and they tie themselves in knots to wipe the slime off and to tear food.
fact: Many deep fish have big eyes to catch faint light from glowing animals, and some have tube-shaped eyes that look upward.
fact: Sperm whales dive into the deep to hunt squid and find them in total darkness with clicks, which are among the loudest sounds made by any animal.
fact: Most of the deep sea floor has never been seen by people.
fact: Only twelve people have walked on the Moon; for a long time even fewer had reached the deepest point of the ocean, but by 2022 more than twenty people had been there, most of them since 2019.
exercise: Why does a deep-sea anglerfish have a glowing lure? | To attract prey to its mouth | To light its way home through the dark | To keep warm in the icy deep water | To scare away whales and sharks
exercise: Where does the light in many anglerfish lures come from? | Glowing bacteria living inside the lure | A tiny battery | Sunlight it stored up during the day near the top | Its eyes
exercise: Why can a gulper eel swallow such big meals? | Its mouth opens very wide and its stomach stretches | It cuts its food into small pieces with sharp claws first | It eats only tiny bits | It has three stomachs
exercise: When was the giant squid first filmed alive in its deep home? | In 2012 | In 1912 | In 1812 | It has never been filmed
exercise: How big are a giant squid's eyes? | About as big as a football | About as big as a garden pea | About as big as a small coin | About as big as a family car
exercise: What does a vampire squid do when threatened? | Flips its webbed arms over its body like a cloak | Bites with its fangs | Sucks the blood of passing fish | Flies right out of the water like a flying fish
exercise: What does the vampire squid really eat? | Drifting bits of dead stuff | The warm blood of passing fish | Whole whales that it drags down | Only living crabs it catches
exercise: Why are dumbo octopuses called dumbo? | Their fins look like elephant ears | They are not very clever | They have little trunks for sucking up food | They are grey like elephants
exercise: How deep do dumbo octopuses live? | Deeper than any other known octopus, at nearly seven kilometres | Only in rock pools | Just under the surface | Only in caves on land
exercise: Why does a blobfish look like a sad blob in photos? | It is hurt when pulled up from the deep | It is always that shape, its whole life | It is very old | It is made of soft jelly all its life, like a jellyfish
exercise: What happens when a whale's body sinks to the deep sea floor? | It feeds deep-sea animals for years | It turns into stone at once in the cold | It floats back up to the top the very next day | Nothing eats it, as it is too deep down
exercise: Where do bone-eating worms live? | Only on whale bones | In people's gardens | On coral reefs | Inside sharks
exercise: What does a hagfish do when attacked? | It makes huge amounts of slime | It explodes with a bang | It turns to stone | It sings
exercise: Why do many deep fish have big eyes? | To catch faint light from glowing animals | To see the Sun far above the water | To look scary | Because they never sleep and need to stay awake
exercise: How does a sperm whale find squid in total darkness? | With loud clicks and their echoes | With a glowing light on its big head | By smell alone, sniffing the water like a shark | It waits for squid to swim into its mouth
exercise: How much of the deep sea floor have people seen? | Only a small part, most has never been seen | All of it, every last bit of it | Exactly half | None of the sea floor at all, not even a little bit
exercise: How many people have walked on the Moon? | Twelve | Two | A hundred | More than a thousand
test: In Finding Nemo a huge anglerfish chases Marlin and Dory with a glowing lure. What is real? | The lure is real, but many deep anglerfish are smaller than a football | Real anglerfish are as big as buses | Real anglerfish use torches | The lure is a cartoon invention
test: «we know more about the Moon than the deep sea, AND more people walked on the Moon than went to the deepest bit» What is right? | The first part is often said, but by 2022 more than twenty people had reached the deepest point, more than the twelve on the Moon | Both are still exactly true | Nobody has ever been to the deepest point | Thousands have walked on the Moon
test: In a cartoon a vampire squid bites a diver's neck to drink blood. What is real? | It eats drifting bits of dead stuff and hides under its cloak of arms | It drinks blood every night | It has fangs like a bat | It lives in old castles on the cliffs and flies out over the sea at night
test: «2 dumbo octopuses n a giant squid, how many arms and tentacles» | 26 | 24 | 18 | 20
test: «4 hagfish all slimed a bucket each, how many buckets of slime» | 4 | 1 | 8 | 2
test: What would happen to a blobfish in its deep home if nobody pulled it up? | It would look like a normal fish | It would melt into a puddle of jelly at once | It would float right up to the surface | It would turn bright pink and then explode
test: «ugliest animal in the world is the blobfish, it's proven» What is right? | That vote used a photo of a fish hurt by being pulled up, so it was not fair | Yes, it looks like that all its life | Yes, scientists measured ugliness | No, the blobfish is a kind of jellyfish
test: Scientists drop a dead whale's body in deep water and come back years later. What will they most likely find? | Animals still living off it, including worms on the bones | Nothing left at all after one day | A whale that came back to life | The same body untouched
test: In Pinocchio a giant whale swallows a boat. Which real whale dives into the deep to hunt giant squid? | The sperm whale, using clicks to find them | The blue whale, using its big eyes in the dark | No whale goes into the deep | Killer whales that only eat boats
test: «hagfish tying itself in a knot, is it ok» What is going on? | It ties knots to wipe off its slime and tear food | It is dying of cold | It is trying to sleep in a knot so it does not drift | It is lost
test: «that eel ate a fish as big as itself, impossible» What is right? | Gulper eels can, because their mouths open very wide and stomachs stretch | Yes, impossible for any fish | It must have been two eels | The fish swam out again
test: A deep fish has huge eyes but lives where sunlight never reaches. Why does it need them? | To catch faint light from glowing animals | It needs them to see the Sun high above | They are left over from its ancestors and useless | To scare people
exam: Identify the source of luminescence in the lure of numerous species of deep-sea anglerfish. | Light-producing bacteria residing within the lure | A store of sunlight absorbed during the day at the surface | Friction of the lure against the water | Reflection of light from the eyes of prey
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the diet of the vampire squid? | It consumes drifting particles of dead organic matter | It subsists upon the blood of fish | It hunts whales of considerable size | It consumes exclusively living crabs captured upon the sea floor
exam: Ascertain the reason for which photographs of the blobfish misrepresent its natural appearance. | The fish is damaged when it is brought up from the great depths it inhabits | It is photographed exclusively in old age | It is invariably photographed while asleep | It is in all circumstances a gelatinous mass
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a whale carcass upon the deep sea floor. | It sustains communities of deep-sea animals for a period of years | It is consumed entirely within one day | It is petrified within a short period | It rises to the surface on the following day and is carried away by the currents
